---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../components/SiteHeader.astro";
import SimpleFooter from "../components/SimpleFooter.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="Privacy Policy - openEHR Explorer"
  description="What data openEHR Explorer stores locally, which network connections it makes, and what the optional, opt-in usage analytics and crash reports contain."
  path="privacy.html"
>
  <SiteHeader current="privacy" />

  <div class="container">
    <div class="page-title">
      <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
      <p>Last updated: 11 October 2026</p>
    </div>

    <section>
      <h2>Summary</h2>
      <p>
        openEHR Explorer is a desktop application that runs entirely on your machine. There is no openEHR Explorer account, no backend service operated
        by this project, and no patient or clinical data is ever sent to us. Other than your own requests to the servers you configure and update
        checks, the only data that can leave your machine is the anonymous analytics described below: a minimal launch ping that is sent even if you
        decline, plus usage and crash data that is sent only if you opt in.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Data stored on your machine</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Server profiles</strong> (name, URL, server type, authentication method) are stored in a local configuration file, for example
          <code>~/.config/openehr-explorer/profiles.json</code> on Linux and macOS or <code>%APPDATA%\openehr-explorer\profiles.json</code> on Windows.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Credentials</strong> are stored in your operating system's keychain, or in an encrypted local file if no keychain is available.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Request Inspector history</strong> is kept in memory for the current session only. Authorization headers and cookies are redacted.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Settings</strong> such as your analytics choice are stored locally.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Network connections</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Servers you configure.</strong> The app sends requests directly from your machine to the openEHR servers (and the optional terminology
          server) you set up. The project never sees this traffic or its contents.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Update checks.</strong> The app checks <a href="https://github.com/platzhersh/openehr-explorer/releases">GitHub Releases</a> for a newer
          version. GitHub receives the request as it would from any download, including your IP address, under
          <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >GitHub's privacy statement</a
          >.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Anonymous analytics</strong> to <a href="https://aptabase.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Aptabase</a>, only in release
          builds that include an Aptabase app key. See below.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="analytics">
      <h2>Anonymous analytics and crash reports</h2>
      <p>
        On first launch the app asks whether you want to share anonymous usage data. Sharing is <strong>off unless you opt in</strong>, and you can change
        your choice at any time under <em>Settings</em>.
      </p>

      <h3>Sent even if you decline</h3>
      <p>
        A single <code>session_started</code> event is sent once per launch, after you have answered the consent dialog. Its only property is your choice
        (<code>consent: "yes"</code> or <code>"no"</code>). It exists so the project can tell how many people use the app compared with how many opt in.
        Aptabase's built-in session metadata, including the app version and operating system, accompanies it. If you quit before answering the dialog,
        nothing is sent.
      </p>

      <h3>Sent only if you opt in</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Usage data</strong>: the app version, operating system and architecture; which features you use (for example that an AQL query was
          run or a composition was viewed); and the CDR platform type (<code>ehrbase</code>, <code>better_platform</code>, <code>ferro_ehr</code> or
          <code>generic</code>).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Crash and error reports</strong>: an error category and a scrubbed code location (a file name and line number). Error messages are
          never sent.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Never sent</h3>
      <p>
        Analytics events never contain server URLs, credentials, patient data, EHR IDs, composition content, query text, template content or anything
        you type. The design is documented in
        <a href="https://github.com/platzhersh/openehr-explorer/blob/main/docs/adr/ADR-0018-aptabase-opt-in-usage-analytics.md">ADR-0018</a> and the
        source code is open for inspection. How Aptabase handles data it receives is described in
        <a href="https://aptabase.com/legal/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">its privacy policy</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Code signing</h2>
      <p>
        Where code signing is enabled for a release, Windows installers are signed with a certificate provided by the
        <a href="https://signpath.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SignPath Foundation</a>. Releases without a signature may show a
        Windows SmartScreen warning. Signing does not involve any user data.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Questions or concerns? Open an issue on <a href="https://github.com/platzhersh/openehr-explorer/issues">GitHub</a>. Changes to this policy are
        tracked in the <a href="https://github.com/platzhersh/openehr-explorer/commits/main/website/src/pages/privacy.astro">page history</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>

  <SimpleFooter />
</Layout>

<style>
  .container {
    max-width: 800px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0 24px 64px;
  }
  .page-title {
    padding: 56px 0 24px;
  }
  .page-title h1 {
    font-size: 40px;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    margin-bottom: 8px;
  }
  .page-title p {
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }
  section {
    margin-top: 32px;
  }
  h2 {
    font-size: 24px;
    margin-bottom: 12px;
  }
  h3 {
    font-size: 17px;
    margin: 20px 0 8px;
  }
  p,
  li {
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    line-height: 1.65;
  }
  ul {
    padding-left: 22px;
    display: grid;
    gap: 8px;
  }
  strong {
    color: var(--text-primary);
  }
</style>
